findings.body:
The new assertion in `EmbeddingServerAppInsideIframe_WithCompressionEnabled_Fails` is meant to accept either the old or the new browser CSP message wording. It is written with `&&`, though. A single log message can only be in one of the two wordings, so the check is false for every real message and the test will always fail.

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerExecutionTests/WebSocketCompressionTests.cs" lines="106-109"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The message text differs between "Refused to frame ..." and "Framing ... violates ...". Whichever wording the browser emits matches exactly one regex, so the AND is always false. The intent is clearly OR, as the failure message says ("match one of the CSP error patterns").

Suggested fix:

::suggestion{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerExecutionTests/WebSocketCompressionTests.cs" lines="106-109"}
Assert.True(
    ParseErrorMessageRegexOld.IsMatch(logs[0].Message) ||
    ParseErrorMessageRegexNew.IsMatch(logs[0].Message),
    $"Expected log message to match one of the CSP error patterns: {ParseErrorMessageRegexOld} or {ParseErrorMessageRegexNew}. Actual: {logs[0].Message}");

In `CanSendAndReceiveUserMessagesUserNameWithPatternIsTreatedAsLiteral`, the two connections are now disposed in the reverse order (secondConnection first, then connection). The PR does not say why, and nothing else in the diff depends on it. The reordering looks like a deliberate test-stability fix, but without a comment it can look like an accidental swap during review, and a future edit could revert it.

::diff{file="src/SignalR/server/StackExchangeRedis/test/RedisEndToEnd.cs" lines="210-214"}

If the order matters (for example, to avoid a reconnect race on the shared Redis backplane), add a short comment saying so. If it does not matter, the swap is noise and should be reverted to keep the diff focused.

The default `condition` in `job/source-index-stage1.yml` changes from empty to `eq(variables['Build.SourceBranch'], 'refs/heads/main')`. Any caller that does not pass its own condition now skips the source-index stage on non-main branches, including `release/9.0`, which is the branch this PR targets.

::diff{file="eng/common/core-templates/job/source-index-stage1.yml" lines="9"}

This is probably an intentional arcade upstream change, and it is a sensible default for source indexing. Since the PR is titled `[release/9.0]`, however, it quietly changes release-branch pipeline behaviour. Confirm that the release/9.0 pipelines either pass their own condition or are fine with source-index being skipped.

The internal source-build runtime download arguments change from `https://dotnetbuilds.blob.core.windows.net/internal` to `https://ci.dot.net/internal`, both for the MSBuild properties and for the `--runtimesourcefeed` CLI argument. The same read token is still used. This is an infrastructure change to where the internal runtime packages come from.

::diff{file="eng/common/core-templates/steps/source-build.yml" lines="44"}

This is most likely part of the arcade sync that moves feeds, and ci.dot.net may be the intended front door. But the PR does not mention it, and a wrong URL would only show up when an internal source-build runs. Ask the build infra owners to confirm that `ci.dot.net/internal` serves the same packages with the same token scope before merging.

In the test-only `WebSocketWrapper.ReceiveAsync`, the `_receiveTcs` reset is moved above the zero-byte early return. Before this change, a zero-byte read returned without resetting the TCS, so the next receive awaited the already-completed previous result and returned stale data. With the reset moved up, each consumed result is cleared regardless of buffer size. The change is small and right.

::code{file="src/SignalR/server/StackExchangeRedis/test/RedisEndToEnd.cs" lines="406-417"}

The wrapper class also drops `sealed`. That is fine for a test helper, but check that nothing relies on the type being sealed.
